  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1253 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nA cold front was situated from Lake Michigan to SE MO into\nwestern AR at 18z. Temperatures are mild ahead of the front, with\nlower 70s common across the region. An initial band of showers\nwas moving across the MO Bootheel and NE AR early this afternoon.\nAdditional development is expected along and ahead of the front\nas it crosses the region through this evening. QPF amounts are\nexpected to be meager as the mid-level shortwave supporting this\nfront weakens as it heads east. Some instability will creep into\nthe Delta region this afternoon. However, weak mid-level lapse\nrates will limit thunder chances. QPF amounts of 0.25 inches or\nless are expected, though a few lucky locations could exceed that\nnumber.\n\nCool and dry air will filter into the region on brisk north winds\nMonday and Tuesday. Temperatures will cool through this period,\nand by Tuesday, expect morning lows in the upper 20s to mid 30s\nwith highs in the upper 40s to mid 50s.\n\nWednesday continues to intrigue. An increasingly negatively\ntilted upper trough moving out of the Red River Valley Tuesday\nnight, combined with a westward moving inverted trough across the\nsouthern states will produce rain across the region. Exactly\nwhere the rain falls will depend on how far west the inverted\ntrough makes it. The latest model runs are trending further west\nthan 24 hours ago, which is bringing 0.25 inch QPF amounts to a\nroughly north-south line running through Jackson, TN, and Oxford,\nMS.  This westward trend of the inverted trough will likely lead\nto warmer temperatures Wednesday morning, though the NBM is slow\nto pick up on this for now.\n\nThere are some pieces of data pointing to the possibility of some\nsnow on the NW side of the precipitation shield early Wednesday\nacross NE AR and the MO Bootheel. The 12z Canadian is among the\nmost aggressive since it is generally colder and deeper with the\nupper system and keeps the inverted surface trough over AL.\nHowever, very few members of the GEFS, EPS, or GEPS are depicting\nany measurable snow. CAMs are becoming available for a look at\nthis system, and the 12z RRFS is quite mild with the inverted\ntrough stretching from north MS into West TN.  Given the universe\nof data, it remains highly unlikely that snowflakes will fly\nacross NE AR and the MO Bootheel.\n\nThe system will depart by Thanksgiving, leaving behind clouds and\ncool temperatures with highs in the lower 50s. Dry weather will\ncontinue through the weekend with gradually moderating\ntemperatures.",
